[
  {
    "episode_id": 75136,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75136.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Last Tuesday, transportation people were above over initial reports that the President and congressional Democrats were getting along over the question of funding for infrastructure. But in the hours and days that followed, the sweet sound of the post-meeting news conference quickly turned into sour commentary over the $2 trillion question. How do we pay the bill? The President's Chief of Staff was clear there would be no tinkering with tax reform. Anti-tax groups warped hard to throw cold water on calls for gas tax increases and raised eyebrows could be seen all over the hill. Another White House meeting is planned for mid-May. If it happens, will it be worth the wait? Is there any way to reach agreement on a plan to pay for road and bridge projects? 43 members of Congress sat at the guest table Wednesday during a hearing of the House Transportation and Infrastructure Committee, each of them sharing their hopes and fears for the work ahead. House Majority Leader Steny Hoyer of Maryland said Americans are eager for an agreement.",
