  {
    "episode_id": 75136,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75136.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "This is Hard Facts. I'm Robert Johnson. As the debate rages over funding transportation projects, the introduction of a bill to enact fiscal review happened quietly a few weeks ago. That's when Congressman Pete Stauber, a Republican from Minnesota, introduced the paid act, requiring states to conduct a lifecycle cost analysis on any project that includes more than $30 million in federal funds. We visited with the Congressman in his office last Thursday. Here's that conversation. So let's just begin talking about the infrastructure news of the year so far, the president's meeting with congressional Democrats at the White House. What's your take on that whole thing?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "This is Hard Facts.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "I am Robert Johnson.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "A bill to enact fiscal review was introduced quietly a few weeks ago.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Congressman Pete Stauber, a Republican from Minnesota, introduced the Paid Act.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The Paid Act requires states to conduct a lifecycle cost analysis on projects exceeding $30 million in federal funds.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The audience expects factual reporting on current legislative developments.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The term 'Hard Facts' signals credibility and objectivity in journalism.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Quietly introducing legislation implies it may lack public attention but still has policy significance.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener will find value in discussing high-level political meetings like the presidential briefing with Democrats.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 75136,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75136.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "I'm glad they're talking with each other. I think that what came out of that meeting is We have definitely the need for an infrastructure project in this country. You know, our federal highways and bridges are 50, 60 years old. It's time that we invest into them and make sure that our roads and bridges continue to be safe. And for me on the transportation and infrastructure committee, that was really good news. There's good things going on. And I am one that really wants to look forward to that investment into our federal infrastructure.",
